  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a221d8ca",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3. Putting tensors (and models) on the GPU\n",
    "The reason we want out tensors/models on the GPU is because using a GPU results in faster computations."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 28,
   "id": "2d7a273f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "tensor([1, 2, 3]) cpu\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Create a tensor (default on the CPU)\n",
    "tensor = torch.tensor([1,2,3], device=\"cpu\")\n",
    "\n",
    "#Tensor on the GPU\n",
    "print(tensor, tensor.device)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 29,
   "id": "0b92e613",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "(tensor([1, 2, 3]), 'cpu')"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 29,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "device = \"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\"\n",
    "tensor_on_gpu = tensor.to(device)\n",
    "tensor_on_gpu, device"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a247b755",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 4. Moving Tensors back to CPU"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 30,
   "id": "c6ee44cb",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "array([1, 2, 3])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 30,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# If you want to use NumPy for your tensors, then you'll have to shift to CPU, because NumPy can't leverage with GPU...\n",
    "tensor_on_gpu.numpy() # -> It will throw an error if you are using CUDA"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "1ae87b1a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "array([1, 2, 3])"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 31,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "tensor_back_on_CPU = tensor_on_gpu.cpu().numpy()\n",
    "tensor_back_on_CPU"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "03b1357c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Summary:\n",
    "Date - 27/Mar/2026\n",
    "\n",
    "--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "  Function                                       Meaning\n",
    "tensor.to(device)             Moves the tensor to the selected device (e.g. GPU)\n",
    "tensor.cpu()                   Moves the tensor from its current device to CPU  \n",
    "tensor.numpy()                      Converts the tensor to a numpy array \n",
    "\n",
    "--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "##### Why we can't use NumPy on GPU?\n",
    "This is because NumPy was made in simpler and older times while GPUs were made in a new era. \n",
    "<br><br>\n",
    "🧠 The real reason\n",
    "\n",
    "NumPy is designed to:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Run on CPU only\n",
    "* Use optimized CPU libraries (BLAS, LAPACK)\n",
    "* Treat memory as regular RAM\n",
    "\n",
    "GPUs? Completely different beast:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Separate memory (VRAM)\n",
    "* Different architecture (thousands of tiny cores)\n",
    "* Needs special parallel code (CUDA, etc.)\n",
    "\n",
    "Numpy just can't talk to GPUs..."
   ]
  }
